// Professional explanatory content: why a marking is ambiguous, and how to
// identify the actual component. Accurate, cautious wording — no fabricated
// standards, no keyword stuffing.
---

<section class="engineering-guide" aria-labelledby="guide-why">
  <div class="container">
    <h2 id="guide-why" data-i18n="guide_why">Why can the same SMD code mean different things?</h2>
    <p>
      A three-digit marking is a compact way to encode a value, but it is used by
      more than one kind of component. A resistor reads the code as resistance in
      ohms; a ceramic capacitor reads the same three digits as capacitance in
      picofarads.
    </p>

    <div class="example-block">
      <p class="example-code" dir="ltr">103</p>
      <dl class="example-values">
        <div class="example-row">
          <dt>Resistor</dt>
          <dd>10 kΩ</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="example-row">
          <dt>Ceramic capacitor</dt>
          <dd>10 nF</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <p>
      Because both readings are valid, the marking alone does not tell you which
      component you are looking at. You need additional context to decide.
    </p>

    <h2 data-i18n="guide_identify">How to identify the actual component</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Component appearance</strong> — resistors and ceramic capacitors
        usually look different. Resistors are commonly flat and dark; ceramic
        capacitors are commonly a lighter tan or brown. Appearance is only a first
        hint.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Package / physical size</strong> — note the part's footprint (for
        example 0402, 0603, 0805) and its thickness. This narrows the possibilities
        but does not confirm them.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Circuit location</strong> — where the part sits is often the
        strongest clue. A capacitor next to a power pin is likely a decoupling
        capacitor; a resistor in series with a signal line is likely a series
        resistor.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Multimeter measurement</strong> — measure it. A multimeter in
        resistance mode confirms a resistor; a capacitance meter confirms a
        capacitor.
      </li>
    </ol>

    <p class="guide-principle">
      Never identify a component by its SMD marking alone. Decode first, then verify.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
